## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical mastery of containerized TLS termination, lifecycle upgrades, and host firewall controls in RH9.

## Key Concepts
- Podman/nginx-like TLS termination
- systemd drop-ins for zero-downtime reloads
- nftables rate limiting
- SELinux policy for container access
- certificate rotation with hot reload

## Code Example
```bash
# container run (example)
podman run -d --name proxy \
  -v /srv/certs:/etc/nginx/certs:ro \
  -p 8443:8443:Z nginx:latest
```

```bash
# systemd drop-in (ExecReload for zero downtime)
[Service]
ExecReload=/bin/kill -HUP $MAINPID
```

```bash
# minimal SELinux module (conceptual)
module proxytls 1.0;
require {
  type container_t;
  class file read; class tcp_socket name_bind; 
}
# allow container_t to read certs and bind 8443
allow container_t cert_dir_t:file read;
allow container_t self:tcp_socket name_bind;
```

```bash
# nftables (inbound 8443 and global rate limit)
table inet proxy {
  chain input {
    type filter hook input priority 0;
    tcp dport 8443 accept
  }
}
table inet limit {
  chain input {
    type filter hook input priority 0;
    limit rate 100/minutediffers
  }
}
```

```bash
# test plan (rotation and rollback)
# 1. load test 100 req/min against https://host:8443
# 2. replace files in /srv/certs/rotated with new certs
# 3. touch a trigger or run systemd reload to apply without downtime
# 4. simulate revocation by replacing with old cert and reloading
```

## Why This Is Asked
This scenario tests practical container containment, SELinux policy implementation, audit trail configuration, and host isolation in a realistic data ingestion workflow.

## Key Concepts
- Podman user namespaces for UID/GID isolation
- SELinux targeted policy modules for domain confinement
- Auditd path monitoring for comprehensive file I/O tracking
- Secure bind mounts with minimal host filesystem exposure

## Code Example
```bash
# Create SELinux module
module ingest_t 1.0;

require {
    type unconfined_t;
    type var_run_t;
    type etc_t;
    type default_t;
    class file { read write open getattr };
    class dir { search read open getattr };
    class sock_file { write open };
}

# Policy rules for ingest_t domain
allow ingest_t etc_t:file { read open getattr };
allow ingest_t var_run_t:sock_file { write open };
allow ingest_t default_t:dir { search read open getattr };
allow ingest_t default_t:file { read write open getattr };
```

## Implementation Commands
```bash
# Create dedicated user
sudo useradd -r -s /bin/false ingest
sudo usermod -v 100000-199999 -w 100000-199999 ingest

# Build and load SELinux module
cat > ingest.te << 'EOF'
module ingest_t 1.0;

require {
    type unconfined_t;
    type var_run_t;
    type etc_t;
    type default_t;
    class file { read write open getattr };
    class dir { search read open getattr };
    class sock_file { write open };
}

allow ingest_t etc_t:file { read open getattr };
allow ingest_t var_run_t:sock_file { write open };
allow ingest_t default_t:dir { search read open getattr };
allow ingest_t default_t:file { read write open getattr };
EOF

checkmodule -M -m -o ingest.mod ingest.te
semodule_package -o ingest.pp -m ingest.mod
sudo semodule -i ingest.pp

# Configure audit rules
sudo auditctl -w /opt/ingest/config -p r -k ingest_config
sudo auditctl -w /srv/ingest/data -p rwxa -k ingest_data
sudo auditctl -w /var/run/ingest-proxy.sock -p w -k ingest_ipc

# Run container
podman run \
  --uidmap 0:100000:65536 \
  --gidmap 0:100000:65536 \
  -v /opt/ingest/config:/opt/ingest/config:ro \
  -v /srv/ingest/data:/srv/ingest/data:rw \
  -v /var/run/ingest-proxy.sock:/var/run/ingest-proxy.sock \
  --network=none \
  --security-opt label=type:ingest_t \
  --user 1000 \
  ingest-service:latest
```
